5470 true] [17592186350812 1145 "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065540/assets/23f31619eda46f1e8604695c4056d4c0c5bb8ee1e186b1316151762d5b5c8e59.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- Functions I and II equal $\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\n- We cannot use function III. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(0,1)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535470 true] [17592186350813 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350814 13194139535470 true] [17592186350813 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350815 13194139535470 true] [17592186350813 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350816 13194139535470 true] [17592186350813 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350817 13194139535470 true] [17592186350813 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350818 13194139535470 true] [17592186350813 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535470 true] [17592186350813 1041 "selection" 13194139535470 true] [17592186350813 1104 #edb/ref 17592186350817 13194139535470 true] [17592186350813 1110 #uuid "89264532-428b-52e8-a27f-a6938b52ab30" 13194139535470 true] [17592186350814 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535470 true] [17592186350814 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535470 true] [17592186350814 1111 #uuid "882b6ae6-35dc-576a-b270-e6c30d8dc303" 13194139535470 true] [17592186350815 1034 "I only" 13194139535470 true] [17592186350815 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535470 true] [17592186350815 1111 #uuid "5885f77f-1c1d-5617-ac43-f56402a4c8b9" 13194139535470 true] [17592186350816 1034 "None"